WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.905
In this last Day 3 demonstration we do two things.

2
00:00:04.905 --> 00:00:08.690
First, sort indicators into IOCs and IOAs.

3
00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:14.727
Second, the method for finding the right MITRE ATT&CK technique ID,

4
00:00:14.727 --> 00:00:20.224
which you will do on the real ATT&CK website in Activity 3.3.

5
00:00:21.323 --> 00:00:26.011
These six indicators all come from the Enrollment Form case.

6
00:00:26.011 --> 00:00:29.604
Remember the two definitions from the handout.

7
00:00:29.604 --> 00:00:36.947
An IOC, indicator of compromise, is evidence that something bad already happened: a file hash,

8
00:00:36.947 --> 00:00:39.135
a known bad IP, a file name.

9
00:00:39.135 --> 00:00:44.915
An IOA, indicator of attack, is behavior that shows an attack in progress.

10
00:00:46.015 --> 00:00:47.319
Here is my sorting.

11
00:00:47.319 --> 00:00:51.093
The blocklisted IP, the scheduled task's name and path,

12
00:00:51.093 --> 00:00:56.926
and the attachment's file name are IOCs: they are artifacts the attacker left behind.

13
00:00:56.926 --> 00:01:04.062
wscript running a script from Downloads, one account logging in from two countries twenty minutes apart,

14
00:01:04.062 --> 00:01:10.376
and wscript starting a hidden PowerShell are IOAs: they describe what the attacker is doing.

15
00:01:11.475 --> 00:01:13.418
Why do analysts love IOAs?

16
00:01:13.418 --> 00:01:19.396
Because the attacker can change an IP address, a file name or a hash in seconds.

17
00:01:19.396 --> 00:01:26.569
But the behavior, an office script engine launching hidden PowerShell, is much harder to change.

18
00:01:26.569 --> 00:01:33.219
A detection rule built on behavior keeps working when the attacker moves to a new server.

19
00:01:34.319 --> 00:01:36.332
Now the ATT&CK method.

20
00:01:36.332 --> 00:01:41.364
On attack dot mitre dot org, open Matrices, Enterprise.

21
00:01:41.364 --> 00:01:45.481
First decide the tactic, the attacker's goal.

22
00:01:45.481 --> 00:01:51.153
Then find the technique in that column, or use the search box.

23
00:01:51.153 --> 00:01:56.917
Copy the ID exactly, including the sub-technique after the dot.

24
00:01:56.917 --> 00:02:03.138
For example, wscript running JavaScript is Execution, T1059 dot 007.

25
00:02:03.138 --> 00:02:08.262
A scheduled task at logon is Persistence, T1053 dot 005.

26
00:02:08.262 --> 00:02:13.934
Calling home over HTTPS is Command and Control, T1071 dot 001.

27
00:02:13.934 --> 00:02:21.527
On the technique page, scroll to Detection and Mitigations for your recommendation.

28
00:02:22.627 --> 00:02:23.518
Your turn.

29
00:02:23.518 --> 00:02:33.043
Activity 3.3 uses the real ATT&CK website in your browser: look up the six behaviors and record the tactic,

30
00:02:33.043 --> 00:02:35.980
ID and name in your Lab Notebook.

31
00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:45.417
Activity 3.4: sort the six indicators into IOC or IOA with your partner, and explain why IOAs last longer.

32
00:02:45.417 --> 00:02:49.867
Both worksheets are in the CTM Virtual Lab, Day 3.

